.TH "IDLEDEADLINE.TIMEREMAINING" "3JS" "June 12, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
IdleDeadline.timeRemaining \- IdleDeadline: timeRemaining() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]timeRemaining()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]IdleDeadline\f[R] interface returns the estimated number of
milliseconds the user agent will remain idle for.
The callback can call this method at any time to determine how much time
it can continue to work before it must return.
For example, if the callback finishes a task and has another one to
begin, it can call \f[CR]timeRemaining()\f[R] to see if there\(cqs
enough time to complete the next task.
If there isn\(cqt, the callback can just return immediately, or look for
other work to do with the remaining time.
.PP
By the time \f[CR]timeRemaining()\f[R] reaches \f[CR]0\f[R], it is
suggested that the callback should return control to the user agent\(cqs
event loop.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] The value returned by \f[CR]timeRemaining()\f[R] is an
estimate of how much time the user agent believes is available before
the next latency\-critical task needs to run.
This estimate is not fixed and can suddenly drop to 0 if
higher\-priority work arrives.
For example, the browser\(cqs estimate can change in the middle of an
idle callback if the user clicks.
Developers should not assume the value always decreases linearly like a
countdown timer.
.RE
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
timeRemaining()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]DOMHighResTimeStamp\f[R] value (which is a floating\-point
number) representing the number of milliseconds the user agent estimates
are left in the current idle period.
The value is ideally accurate to within about 5 microseconds.
.PP
If the \f[CR]IdleDeadline\f[R] object\(cqs \f[CR]didTimeout\f[R]
property is true, this method returns zero.
.SH EXAMPLES
See our complete example in the article Cooperative Scheduling of
Background Tasks API.
